WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.680
Missing security requirements leave teams without clear expectations for tenant isolation,

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:10.340
authentication, authorization, auditability, data retention, rate limits,

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.640
and administrative workflows.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:17.900
Unsafe workflows allow attackers to manipulate business logic even when individual

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.960
endpoints behave as coded.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.740
SaaS products need sequence enforcement, approval checks, abuse prevention,

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.640
and state validation.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:31.340
Threat modeling helps teams identify likely abuse paths before code is deployed.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.880
It is most useful when it covers trust boundaries, sensitive data flows,

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.980
privileged actions, and integrations.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.240
Architectural weaknesses often come from misplaced trust in internal networks,

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:48.220
client-side state, third-party callbacks, or service-to-service calls.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.360
SOC 2 readiness benefits from documented design decisions and risk handling.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.780
Use threat modeling for critical workflows and trust boundaries.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.760
Integrate secure SDLC practices into design and development.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:03.180
Perform security architecture reviews for high-risk features.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:09.160
Selected SOC 2 mappings for this topic include CC3.2 - Risk identification;

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:12.980
CC8.1 - Change management processes.
